==> winch doc project
project: project files and directories

A project is a directory with a winch.yaml file and the inventories,
pipelines, and recipes that it uses. winch reads the project in the
current directory.

Minimal project:
  One winch.yaml with a name, a host, and one step:

    # winch.yaml
    name: demo
    hosts:
      web1.example.com:
        address: 192.0.2.10
    steps:
      - name: Write the message of the day
        file:
          path: /etc/motd
          content: "Managed by winch.\n"

  Check it, show the changes, then make them:

    winch validate
    winch plan
    winch apply

    # winch writes /etc/motd on web1.example.com.

Hosts:
  winch connects with the OpenSSH client and its SSH config. The login
  user is root, or has passwordless sudo. A host needs nothing else
  installed: winch sends a helper program to /var/lib/winch/ on the
  host and runs it there. Run winch doc remote.

When the project grows:
  Move the hosts into inventories, the steps into pipelines, and
  groups of steps into recipes:

    winch.yaml               the project file
    inventories/
      production.yaml        an inventory: hosts and groups
      test.yaml
    pipelines/
      site.yaml              a pipeline: stages and steps
    recipes/
      nginx/
        recipe.yaml          a recipe: variables, steps, and handlers
        templates/site.tmpl  the templates of the recipe
        files/tls.conf       the files of the recipe

  The name of an inventory or a pipeline is its file name without
  .yaml. Plan and apply then name the pipeline: winch plan site.

  An inventory, a pipeline, or a recipe can also be a key in
  winch.yaml, under inventories, pipelines, or recipes. The value of
  the key is the content of the file. A name in both places is an
  error.

Top-level inventory and pipeline:
  hosts and groups at the top of winch.yaml are the only inventory of
  the project. They work with any number of pipelines. steps,
  handlers, parallelism, and health at the top of winch.yaml are the
  only pipeline. Then plan and apply take no pipeline name.

YAML:
  All files are YAML 1.2. Only true and false are booleans: yes, no,
  on, and off are strings. File modes are strings: mode: "0644".
  Unknown keys and duplicate keys are errors. Anchors and aliases
  work. Merge keys (<<) do not. winch fmt fixes the indentation and
  the step key order.

Keys:
  name         string                 required  letters, digits, ., _, and -
  inventory    string                 -         default for --inventory
  recipients   list of strings        -         age public keys
  vars         map                    -         shared variable defaults
  inventories  map                    -         inventories kept in winch.yaml
  pipelines    map                    -         pipelines kept in winch.yaml
  recipes      map                    -         recipes kept in winch.yaml
  hosts        map                    -         hosts of the only inventory
  groups       map                    -         groups of the only inventory
  steps        list of steps          -         steps of the only pipeline
  handlers     map of lists of steps  -         handlers of the only pipeline
  parallelism  integer                -         parallelism of the only pipeline
  health       map                    -         health gate of the only pipeline

Run "winch doc project.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc project.name
project.name: string, required

Letters, digits, ., _, and -. It does not start with a dot. Hosts keep
pending handlers and other state of the project under this name.
==> winch doc project.inventory
project.inventory: string

Default for --inventory. A project with more than one inventory and no
default makes every run name its inventory with --inventory.
==> winch doc project.recipients
project.recipients: list of strings

Age public keys. winch encrypts each secret for all of them. winch
secret init adds a key.
==> winch doc project.vars
project.vars: map

Shared variable defaults, by name. A default can be a !secret, an !expr,
or a !tpl. Run winch doc variables.
==> winch doc project.inventories
project.inventories: map

Inventories kept in winch.yaml, by name. The value of each is the
content of an inventory file, as in inventories/<name>.yaml.
==> winch doc project.pipelines
project.pipelines: map

Pipelines kept in winch.yaml, by name. The value of each is the content
of a pipeline file, as in pipelines/<name>.yaml.
==> winch doc project.recipes
project.recipes: map

Recipes kept in winch.yaml, by name. The value of each is the content of
a recipe file, as in recipes/<name>/recipe.yaml. Such a recipe has no
templates/ or files/ directory. Its steps cannot use template, source,
or the key of repo.
==> winch doc project.hosts
project.hosts: map

Hosts of the only inventory, as in an inventory file. The project then
has no other inventory. It can have any number of pipelines.
==> winch doc project.groups
project.groups: map

Groups of the only inventory, as in an inventory file.
==> winch doc project.steps
project.steps: list of steps

Steps of the only pipeline. It has one stage on all hosts, and both have
the name of the project. The project then has no other pipeline. Plan
and apply take no pipeline name.
==> winch doc project.handlers
project.handlers: map of lists of steps

Handlers of the only pipeline, as in a pipeline file.
==> winch doc project.parallelism
project.parallelism: integer

Parallelism of the only pipeline, as in a pipeline file.
==> winch doc project.health
project.health: map

Health gate of the only pipeline, as in a pipeline file.
==> winch doc inventory
inventory: hosts and groups

An inventory lists the hosts that winch manages, puts them in groups,
and gives them values.

Files:
  An inventory is a file in inventories/, such as
  inventories/production.yaml, or a key in inventories of winch.yaml.
  It holds data and secrets, but no templates or expressions.

Choosing the inventory:
  --inventory <name> names the inventory of a run. inventory in
  winch.yaml names a default. A project with one inventory uses it. A
  project with more inventories and no default makes each run name
  its inventory, so a run against production is always deliberate.

Groups and values:
  The group all contains every host. A host value beats a group
  value, and a group value beats a value of all. Two groups of one
  host that set different values for one variable, or for user, are
  an error, unless the host sets that value too.

SSH settings:
  Every other SSH setting, such as the port or the key, comes from your
  SSH config. Its Host blocks match the host name, also when the host
  has an address.

Host patterns:
  Host patterns select hosts in the hosts of a stage, in --hosts, and
  in winch run and winch exec:

    web          the hosts in the group web, or the host named web
    web,db       the hosts in web or in db
    web & prod   the hosts in web and in prod
    web & !web3  the hosts in web, without web3

  & and ! bind tighter than ",". Hosts keep inventory order. A name
  that is neither a group nor a host is an error. A pattern with only
  ! names is an error: write all & !web, not !web.

Keys:
  hosts          map              -  keyed by host name
  hosts.address  string           -  host name or IP for SSH
  hosts.user     string           -  SSH login user
  hosts.vars     map              -  variable values of the host
  groups         map              -  keyed by group name
  groups.user    string           -  SSH login user of the members
  groups.hosts   list of strings  -  member host names
  groups.vars    map              -  variable values of the members

Example:
  # inventories/production.yaml
  hosts:
    mail.acme.com:
      address: 192.0.2.10
    monitor.acme.com:
      address: 192.0.2.20
  groups:
    all:
      user: admin
      vars:
        domain: acme.com
    mail:
      hosts: [mail.acme.com]
      vars:
        mail_server: true

  # winch.yaml
  name: acme
  vars:
    domain: example.com
    mail_server: false
  steps:
    - name: Install postfix on the mail servers
      pkg:
        name: postfix
      when: !expr vars.mail_server

Run "winch doc inventory.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc inventory.hosts
inventory.hosts: map

Keyed by host name. Without address, the name is the SSH host name. A
host name that YAML reads as a number, such as 1234, needs quotes. A
host and a group cannot have the same name.

Keys:
  address  string  -  host name or IP for SSH
  user     string  -  SSH login user
  vars     map     -  variable values of the host
==> winch doc inventory.hosts.address
inventory.hosts.address: string

Host name or IP for SSH. Without it, the SSH config decides the host
name. The Host blocks of the SSH config match the host name, also with
address. A port goes in the SSH config. Two hosts cannot have the same
address.
==> winch doc inventory.hosts.user
inventory.hosts.user: string

SSH login user. Without it, the host takes the user of its groups, then
of all, then of the SSH config. --user beats it. Every other SSH setting
comes from your SSH config.
==> winch doc inventory.hosts.vars
inventory.hosts.vars: map

Variable values of the host. They beat the values of its groups.
==> winch doc inventory.groups
inventory.groups: map

Keyed by group name. The group all contains every host. A host value
beats a group value, and a group value beats a value of all.

Keys:
  user   string           -  SSH login user of the members
  hosts  list of strings  -  member host names
  vars   map              -  variable values of the members
==> winch doc inventory.groups.user
inventory.groups.user: string

SSH login user of the members. Two groups of one host with different
users are an error, unless the host sets user.
==> winch doc inventory.groups.hosts
inventory.groups.hosts: list of strings

Member host names. The group all cannot have hosts. A group can be
empty: hosts: [].
==> winch doc inventory.groups.vars
inventory.groups.vars: map

Variable values of the members. Two groups of one host that set
different values for one variable are an error, unless the host sets
that value too.
==> winch doc pipeline
pipeline: ordered stages that apply steps to hosts

A pipeline is an ordered list of stages. Each stage applies its steps
to a set of hosts.

Files:
  A pipeline is a file in pipelines/, such as pipelines/site.yaml, or
  a key in pipelines of winch.yaml. winch plan site and winch apply
  site run it.

One stage or many:
  Most pipelines run the same steps on every host. Such a pipeline
  holds the steps directly. It is one stage with the name of the
  pipeline. A pipeline whose hosts have different roles, or that must
  finish on some hosts before it starts on others, has stages.

Order:
  Stages run in order. A stage starts when the previous stage finished
  on all its hosts. Inside a stage, each host runs its steps in order,
  without waiting for other hosts. A stage that matches no host is
  skipped. --stages runs only some stages, and --hosts only some
  hosts.

Steps of a stage:
  A step in a stage can use a module directly. It cannot use template,
  source, or the key of repo: those read the files of a recipe.

Failures:
  A failed host takes no part in later stages. The other hosts go on.
  A new run is the resume: steps that already succeeded check as ok.

Keys:
  stages                 list of maps           -         stages in run order
  stages.name            string                 required  unique in the pipeline
  stages.hosts           string                 all       host pattern
  stages.parallelism     integer                -         maximum hosts at the same time
  stages.health          map                    -         check after a host changed in the stage
  stages.health.command  string                 required  must exit 0
  stages.health.stable   integer                0         seconds the command keeps passing
  stages.health.timeout  integer                60        seconds the gate has to pass
  stages.steps           list of steps          required  recipe steps and module steps, in order
  stages.handlers        map of lists of steps  -         handlers of the stage
  hosts                  string                 all       host pattern of a pipeline without stages
  parallelism            integer                -         maximum hosts at the same time
  health                 map                    -         check after a host changed in the stage
  health.command         string                 required  must exit 0
  health.stable          integer                0         seconds the command keeps passing
  health.timeout         integer                60        seconds the gate has to pass
  steps                  list of steps          -         steps of a pipeline without stages
  handlers               map of lists of steps  -         handlers of a pipeline without stages

Example:
  # pipelines/site.yaml
  stages:
    - name: base
      hosts: all
      parallelism: 1
      steps:
        - timezone:
            name: Etc/UTC
    - name: web
      hosts: web
      health:
        command: systemctl is-active nginx
        stable: 10
        timeout: 120
      steps:
        - recipe: nginx

  # winch.yaml
  name: acme
  inventories:
    production:
      hosts:
        web1.acme.com: {}
        web2.acme.com: {}
      groups:
        web:
          hosts: [web1.acme.com, web2.acme.com]
  recipes:
    nginx:
      steps:
        - pkg:
            name: nginx

Run "winch doc pipeline.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc pipeline.stages
pipeline.stages: list of maps

Stages in run order. A stage starts when the previous stage finished on
all its hosts. A pipeline with stages has no other key.

Keys of each element:
  name         string                 required  unique in the pipeline
  hosts        string                 all       host pattern
  parallelism  integer                -         maximum hosts at the same time
  health       map                    -         check after a host changed in the stage
  steps        list of steps          required  recipe steps and module steps, in order
  handlers     map of lists of steps  -         handlers of the stage
==> winch doc pipeline.stages.name
pipeline.stages.name: string, required

Unique in the pipeline. It follows the rule of host names. --stages
selects stages by name.
==> winch doc pipeline.stages.hosts
pipeline.stages.hosts: string, default all

Host pattern. Run winch doc inventory for the patterns.
==> winch doc pipeline.stages.parallelism
pipeline.stages.parallelism: integer

Maximum hosts at the same time. Hosts start in inventory order. The next
host starts when a host finishes the stage and its health gate passes. A
failure stops new hosts from starting. Running hosts finish the stage.
==> winch doc pipeline.stages.health
pipeline.stages.health: map

Check after a host changed in the stage. The gate runs on a host after
it finishes the stage, when the stage has a change on that host. The
plan marks it with * and does not run it. A gate that fails stays
pending on the host, and the next apply runs it.

Keys:
  command  string   required  must exit 0
  stable   integer  0         seconds the command keeps passing
  timeout  integer  60        seconds the gate has to pass
==> winch doc pipeline.stages.health.command
pipeline.stages.health.command: string, required

Must exit 0. winch runs it on the host once per second until it exits 0,
then keeps running it for stable seconds. A failed run starts the wait
again.
==> winch doc pipeline.stages.health.stable
pipeline.stages.health.stable: integer, default 0

Seconds the command keeps passing. It is less than timeout.
==> winch doc pipeline.stages.health.timeout
pipeline.stages.health.timeout: integer, default 60

Seconds the gate has to pass. Then the host fails, and the error shows
the end of the last output.
==> winch doc pipeline.stages.steps
pipeline.stages.steps: list of steps, required

Recipe steps and module steps, in order.
==> winch doc pipeline.stages.handlers
pipeline.stages.handlers: map of lists of steps

Handlers of the stage, as in a recipe. A module step of the stage
notifies them.
==> winch doc pipeline.hosts
pipeline.hosts: string, default all

Host pattern of a pipeline without stages. Run winch doc inventory for
the patterns.
==> winch doc pipeline.parallelism
pipeline.parallelism: integer

Maximum hosts at the same time. Hosts start in inventory order. The next
host starts when a host finishes the stage and its health gate passes. A
failure stops new hosts from starting. Running hosts finish the stage.
==> winch doc pipeline.health
pipeline.health: map

Check after a host changed in the stage. The gate runs on a host after
it finishes the stage, when the stage has a change on that host. The
plan marks it with * and does not run it. A gate that fails stays
pending on the host, and the next apply runs it.

Keys:
  command  string   required  must exit 0
  stable   integer  0         seconds the command keeps passing
  timeout  integer  60        seconds the gate has to pass
==> winch doc pipeline.health.command
pipeline.health.command: string, required

Must exit 0. winch runs it on the host once per second until it exits 0,
then keeps running it for stable seconds. A failed run starts the wait
again.
==> winch doc pipeline.health.stable
pipeline.health.stable: integer, default 0

Seconds the command keeps passing. It is less than timeout.
==> winch doc pipeline.health.timeout
pipeline.health.timeout: integer, default 60

Seconds the gate has to pass. Then the host fails, and the error shows
the end of the last output.
==> winch doc pipeline.steps
pipeline.steps: list of steps

Steps of a pipeline without stages: recipe steps and module steps, in
order.
==> winch doc pipeline.handlers
pipeline.handlers: map of lists of steps

Handlers of a pipeline without stages, as in a recipe. A module step of
the stage notifies them.
==> winch doc recipe
recipe: reusable groups of steps

A recipe is a named group of steps that you can use again, with its own
variables, templates, and files.

Files:
  A recipe is a directory in recipes/ with a recipe.yaml, and optional
  templates/ and files/ directories. Or it is a key in recipes of
  winch.yaml, without templates or files. winch loads every recipe for
  every command, also a recipe that no pipeline uses.

Names:
  A recipe name has lowercase letters, digits, and _. It can be the
  name of a module.

Running a recipe:
  A step runs a recipe with recipe: <name>. A recipe step has only
  name, recipe, vars, and when. A recipe runs at most once per host in
  a run. Two stages can run the same recipe when their hosts do not
  overlap. A recipe cannot run another recipe.

Templates and files:
  template reads from the templates/ directory of the recipe, and
  source from files/. Paths can name subdirectories. A template file
  uses the language of !tpl. Run winch doc templates.

Keys:
  require   list of expressions    -         conditions on facts
  vars      map                    -         defaults of the recipe's own variables
  steps     list of steps          required  module steps, in order
  handlers  map of lists of steps  -         steps that run after a change

Example:
  # recipes/nginx/recipe.yaml
  require:
    - !expr facts.os.id in ["debian", "ubuntu"]
  vars:
    nginx_port: 80
  steps:
    - pkg:
        name: nginx
    - name: Configure the site
      file:
        path: /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/default
        template: site.tmpl
        validate: nginx -t
      notify: reload
    - service:
        name: nginx
        running: true
        enabled: true
  handlers:
    reload:
      - service:
          name: nginx
          reloaded: true

  # recipes/nginx/templates/site.tmpl
  server {
      listen {{ .vars.nginx_port }};
  }

  # winch.yaml
  name: acme
  hosts:
    web1.acme.com: {}
  steps:
    - recipe: nginx
      vars:
        nginx_port: 8080

Run "winch doc recipe.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc recipe.require
recipe.require: list of expressions

Conditions on facts, each an !expr. Every host where the recipe runs in
the pipeline meets all of them, or it fails at plan. They see only
facts.
==> winch doc recipe.vars
recipe.vars: map

Defaults of the recipe's own variables. Their names start with the
recipe name and _.
==> winch doc recipe.steps
recipe.steps: list of steps, required

Module steps, in order. A recipe cannot run another recipe.
==> winch doc recipe.handlers
recipe.handlers: map of lists of steps

Steps that run after a change. They run after the last step of the
recipe, when a step notified them. Run winch doc handlers.
==> winch doc step
step: keys that every step has

A step is one action on a host: a module with its arguments, or a
recipe.

Form:
  A step is a map. It has one module key, such as pkg or file, with the
  arguments of the module as a map. Or it has recipe with the name of
  a recipe. Run winch doc <module> for the arguments of a module.

Computed values:
  Any module argument can be a template or an expression, and so can
  then. when takes an expression. loop takes a list or an expression.

Evaluation:
  On each host, winch evaluates when. When it is false, winch skips the
  step. Then winch evaluates loop, and the arguments for each element.
  winch checks the step on the host, and apply changes what differs.

Results:
  A step that runs a command counts as changed: a cmd, and a service
  with restarted or reloaded. When a step fails, its host stops. Other
  hosts go on.

Key order:
  winch fmt puts the step keys in this order: name, the module or
  recipe, vars, when, loop, then, confirm_access, notify.

Keys:
  name            string                     -      label in the plan and the logs
  recipe          string                     -      recipe to run, in place of a module
  vars            map                        -      variable values for this recipe step
  when            expression                 -      skips the step when false
  loop            list or expression         -      elements to run the step for, as item
  then            string                     -      command after a change
  confirm_access  boolean                    false  SSH check with rollback, for a service step
  notify          string or list of strings  -      handlers to run after a change

Example:
  # winch.yaml
  name: demo
  hosts:
    web1.example.com: {}
  vars:
    sites: [blog, shop]
  steps:
    - name: Install nginx
      pkg:
        name: nginx
    - name: Write the sites
      file:
        path: !tpl /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/{{ .item }}.conf
        content: !tpl "# {{ .item }}\n"
      when: !expr len(vars.sites) > 0
      loop: !expr vars.sites
      then: nginx -t
      notify: reload
  handlers:
    reload:
      - service:
          name: nginx
          reloaded: true

Run "winch doc step.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc step.name
step.name: string

Label in the plan and the logs. Without it, the plan shows the module
and its main argument.
==> winch doc step.recipe
step.recipe: string

Recipe to run, in place of a module. A recipe step has only name,
recipe, vars, and when. A recipe runs at most once per host in a run.
==> winch doc step.vars
step.vars: map

Variable values for this recipe step. They beat the inventory, and --set
beats them. Only a recipe step has vars.
==> winch doc step.when
step.when: expression

Skips the step when false. It is an !expr that gives true or false.
winch evaluates none of the other keys of a skipped step. when runs
once, before loop, and cannot use item.
==> winch doc step.loop
step.loop: list or expression

Elements to run the step for, as item. An !expr gives the list. loop: []
runs nothing and notifies nothing. A null element fails the host.
==> winch doc step.then
step.then: string

Command after a change. It runs right after the step changes something,
before the next step. A failure of then fails the step. With loop, then
runs once after the last element and cannot use item. A then that did
not run stays pending on the host, and the next run runs it.
==> winch doc step.confirm_access
step.confirm_access: boolean, default false

SSH check with rollback, for a service step. Only a service step can use
it, in steps or in handlers. A firewall step gets the same check without
it.

Before the step, winch keeps the old state of each path that the changed
steps of the recipe wrote or removed in this run. It starts a 60-second
rollback timer in a process of its own.

After the step, winch opens a new SSH connection and runs a command,
through sudo -n when the login user is not root. It tries twice, 5
seconds apart.

When both tries fail, winch puts back the old files and runs
daemon-reload after a unit file. It reverses a start or a stop of the
step, and reloads or restarts the unit again. The host fails with the
error "no new SSH connection works: <error>; the old files are back".
When the SSH connection ends first, winch rolls back as it ends. When
the process of winch on the host is gone, the timer rolls back.

When a run ends before a notified handler with confirm_access ran, winch
puts back the files of that handler at once. The next run applies the
change again.

A rollback puts back files only. It does not undo a change of a user or
a group.
==> winch doc step.notify
step.notify: string or list of strings

Handlers to run after a change. Not a template or an expression. With
loop, notify fires once when any element changed. Run winch doc
handlers.
==> winch doc handlers
handlers: steps that run after a change

A handler is a list of steps that runs once, after a step that notifies
it changes something.

Declaring a handler:
  handlers is a map in a recipe or in a stage. Each key is a handler
  name, and its value is a list of steps. A handler name has lowercase
  letters, digits, and _.

Notifying a handler:
  notify on a step names a handler of the current recipe or stage, or
  <recipe>.<handler> of another recipe. That recipe must run in the
  same pipeline, on at least one host where the step runs. On a host
  where it does not run, winch drops the notification.

    # winch.yaml
    name: demo
    hosts:
      web1.example.com: {}
    steps:
      - recipe: nginx
      - file:
          path: /etc/nginx/conf.d/gzip.conf
          content: "gzip on;\n"
        notify: nginx.reload
    recipes:
      nginx:
        steps:
          - pkg:
              name: nginx
        handlers:
          reload:
            - service:
                name: nginx
                reloaded: true

    # A change of gzip.conf reloads nginx once, at the end of the stage.

When a handler runs:
  - A handler runs at most once per recipe run on a host. A handler of
    a stage runs at most once per stage run.
  - A handler runs after the last step of its recipe. When its recipe
    already ran on that host, it runs at the end of the current stage.
  - A handler of a stage runs after the last step of the stage and
    after the recipe handlers at the end of the stage. It runs before
    the health gate.
  - Handlers run in the order that the recipe or the stage declares
    them.

Handler steps:
  A handler step can have when. It cannot use notify or run a recipe.
  Only a handler step and winch run can use restarted and reloaded of
  service.

Pending handlers:
  When a step notifies a handler, winch marks the handler as pending on
  the host before the step changes anything. The mark goes when the
  handler succeeds. When a run stops first, the handler stays pending.
  It runs the next time its recipe or stage runs on that host. The
  plan shows a handler that will run with "!".
==> winch doc variables
variables: named values for hosts, groups, and recipes

Variables are named values that steps, templates, and expressions
read.

Sources:
  Each host has its own value. A value comes from these sources. A
  later source replaces an earlier one:

    1. the default
    2. the vars of the groups of the host, then of the host
    3. the vars of the recipe step
    4. --set vars.<name>=<value>

  Maps and lists do not merge.

Defaults:
  A default is declared once: in vars of winch.yaml, or in vars of the
  recipe that owns it. A recipe's own defaults start with the recipe
  name and _. A default can be a !secret, a template, or an
  expression. It sees vars, host, and facts, but not match(). A cycle
  between defaults is an error.

Variables without a default:
  A variable without a default needs no declaration. A read of it is
  enough, and the variable is required where it is read. A variable
  that may stay unset needs a default, such as relay_host: "".

Names and reads:
  A variable name has lowercase letters, digits, and _. A read names
  the variable: vars.domain, .vars.domain, $.vars.domain, or
  host.vars.domain. Any other read of the whole map, such as
  keys(vars) or range .vars, is an error. A map variable holds data
  that a step reads by key: vars.users[item]. host.vars holds the
  variables of the host without the vars of a recipe step.

Types:
  Variables have no types. A value keeps the type that YAML gives it.
  --set parses its value as YAML. Quote a value that YAML would
  change: --set 'vars.php_version="8.0"'.

Example:
  # winch.yaml
  name: demo
  hosts:
    mail.acme.com:
      vars:
        relay_host: smtp.acme.com
    web1.acme.com: {}
  groups:
    all:
      vars:
        admin_email: ops@acme.com
  vars:
    domain: acme.com
    relay_host: ""
  steps:
    - recipe: motd
      vars:
        motd_text: Managed by winch
  recipes:
    motd:
      vars:
        motd_text: Welcome
      steps:
        - file:
            path: /etc/motd
            content: !tpl "{{ .vars.motd_text }} on {{ .vars.domain }}\n"
        - file:
            path: /etc/admin_email
            content: !tpl "{{ .vars.admin_email }}\n"
        - file:
            path: /etc/relay_host
            content: !tpl "{{ .vars.relay_host }}\n"
          when: !expr vars.relay_host != ""
==> winch doc templates
templates: text with values filled in (!tpl)

A template is text in which winch fills in the values of each host.

Syntax:
  The !tpl tag marks a template, a Go text/template. It builds text,
  such as a path or a line. The template files of a recipe use the
  same language.

Data:
  A template reads these names with a leading dot:

    .vars   the variables of the host, with the vars of the recipe step
    .facts  the facts of the host; run winch doc facts
    .host   name, short, address, groups, and vars of the host
    .item   the current element in a step with loop

  Inside range, "." is the element. $.vars reads the variables.

Where templates go:
  Any module argument can be a template or an expression, and so can
  then.

Functions and actions:
  A function takes its arguments after its name, separated by spaces:
  {{ join .vars.names ", " }}. Templates also have the actions of Go's
  text/template: if, else, range, with, define, template, block,
  break, and continue, and the variables $x := value.

Missing values:
  A template that reads a missing map key fails. Use get for a key
  that may be missing. A template that prints a null value fails.

Quoting:
  A value goes into a command as it is. Quote a value that must stay
  one word with quote. Quote a YAML value that starts with a quote, [,
  {, &, *, or !, or that contains ": " or " #". A template that starts
  with {{ needs quotes.

When they run:
  winch evaluates templates on the machine that runs winch, during the
  plan. They cannot read the result of a step. winch checks each one
  when it loads the project: an unknown name or a syntax error is an
  error. An error while winch evaluates one for a host fails that
  host. The other hosts go on.

Functions:
  and       and x y: the first empty argument, or the last argument.
  call      call f args: the result of the function f.
  eq        eq a b: true when a equals b.
  ge        ge a b: true when a >= b.
  get       get m key: the value of key in the map m, or nothing when m has no such key.
  gt        gt a b: true when a > b.
  has       has list v: true when list contains v.
  html      html s: s with the special characters of HTML escaped.
  index     index x k: the element of the map or the list x at the key or the index k.
  join      join list sep: the elements of list as text, joined with sep.
  js        js s: s escaped for a JavaScript string.
  json      json v: v as JSON on one line.
  le        le a b: true when a <= b.
  len       len x: the length of a string, a list, or a map.
  lt        lt a b: true when a < b.
  ne        ne a b: true when a does not equal b.
  not       not x: true when x is empty or false.
  or        or x y: the first argument that is not empty, or the last argument.
  print     print args: the arguments as text, as Go's fmt.Sprint gives them.
  printf    printf format args: the arguments formatted as Go's fmt.Sprintf does: {{ printf "%05d" .vars.uid }}.
  println   println args: the arguments as text, with spaces between them and a newline at the end.
  quote     quote s: s in single quotes for sh, each ' as '\''.
  slice     slice x i j: the part of the string or the list x from the index i up to j.
  sqlquote  sqlquote s: s as a MariaDB string literal: in single quotes, each ' as '', each \ as \\.
  title     title s: s with the first letter of each word in upper case.
  urlquery  urlquery s: s escaped for the query of a URL.

Example:
  # winch.yaml
  name: demo
  hosts:
    web1.acme.com: {}
  vars:
    sites: [blog, shop]
    admins: [ann, bob]
  steps:
    - name: Write the sites
      file:
        path: !tpl /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/{{ .item }}.conf
        content: !tpl "server_name {{ .item }}.{{ .host.short }};\n"
      loop: !expr vars.sites
    - name: Allow the admins
      file:
        path: /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/admins.conf
        content: !tpl "AllowUsers{{ range .vars.admins }} {{ . }}{{ end }}\n"

Run "winch doc templates.<function>" for the full text of a function.
==> winch doc templates.and
templates.and: function

and x y: the first empty argument, or the last argument. In an
expression, and is an operator.
==> winch doc templates.call
templates.call: function

call f args: the result of the function f. Project data holds no
functions.
==> winch doc templates.eq
templates.eq: function

eq a b: true when a equals b. With more arguments, true when a equals
any of them. In an expression, use ==.
==> winch doc templates.ge
templates.ge: function

ge a b: true when a >= b.
==> winch doc templates.get
templates.get: function

get m key: the value of key in the map m, or nothing when m has no such
key. It reads a key that a map may leave out: {{ with get . "endpoint"
}}Endpoint = {{ . }}{{ end }}.
==> winch doc templates.gt
templates.gt: function

gt a b: true when a > b.
==> winch doc templates.has
templates.has: function

has list v: true when list contains v. In an expression, use v in list.
==> winch doc templates.html
templates.html: function

html s: s with the special characters of HTML escaped.
==> winch doc templates.index
templates.index: function

index x k: the element of the map or the list x at the key or the index
k. More keys read deeper. A missing map key is an error. Use get for a
key that may be missing.
==> winch doc templates.join
templates.join: function

join list sep: the elements of list as text, joined with sep.
==> winch doc templates.js
templates.js: function

js s: s escaped for a JavaScript string.
==> winch doc templates.json
templates.json: function

json v: v as JSON on one line. Map keys are in sorted order, and
non-ASCII characters stay UTF-8. The output is also a valid YAML value,
so it writes a string into a JSON or a YAML file: "data-root": {{ json
.vars.docker_root }}.
==> winch doc templates.le
templates.le: function

le a b: true when a <= b.
==> winch doc templates.len
templates.len: function

len x: the length of a string, a list, or a map.
==> winch doc templates.lt
templates.lt: function

lt a b: true when a < b.
==> winch doc templates.ne
templates.ne: function

ne a b: true when a does not equal b.
==> winch doc templates.not
templates.not: function

not x: true when x is empty or false. In an expression, not and ! are
operators.
==> winch doc templates.or
templates.or: function

or x y: the first argument that is not empty, or the last argument. In
an expression, or is an operator.
==> winch doc templates.print
templates.print: function

print args: the arguments as text, as Go's fmt.Sprint gives them.
==> winch doc templates.printf
templates.printf: function

printf format args: the arguments formatted as Go's fmt.Sprintf does: {{
printf "%05d" .vars.uid }}.
==> winch doc templates.println
templates.println: function

println args: the arguments as text, with spaces between them and a
newline at the end.
==> winch doc templates.quote
templates.quote: function

quote s: s in single quotes for sh, each ' as '\''. A value goes into a
command as it is. Quote a value that must stay one word: then: !tpl
"postmap {{ quote .vars.map }}".
==> winch doc templates.slice
templates.slice: function

slice x i j: the part of the string or the list x from the index i up to
j. In an expression, use x[i:j].
==> winch doc templates.sqlquote
templates.sqlquote: function

sqlquote s: s as a MariaDB string literal: in single quotes, each ' as
'', each \ as \\. For PostgreSQL, write E before it. It assumes the
default SQL mode, without NO_BACKSLASH_ESCAPES, and a UTF-8 client.
==> winch doc templates.title
templates.title: function

title s: s with the first letter of each word in upper case.
==> winch doc templates.urlquery
templates.urlquery: function

urlquery s: s escaped for the query of a URL.
==> winch doc expressions
expressions: computed values (!expr)

An expression computes a value for each host, such as a list, a
number, or true or false.

Syntax:
  The !expr tag marks an expression in the language of expr
  (expr-lang.org). Its result keeps its type: a list, a number, a
  boolean, a map, or a string.

Data:
  An expression reads these names without a leading dot:

    vars      the variables of the host, with the vars of the recipe step
    facts     the facts of the host; run winch doc facts
    host      name, short, address, groups, and vars of the host
    item      the current element in a step with loop
    match(p)  the hosts that match the host pattern p, with the fields
              of host

  match(p) reads the inventory, not the --hosts selection. A host from
  match() has only the vars that need no facts. A default cannot use
  match().

Where expressions go:
  Any module argument can be an expression or a template, and so can
  then. when takes an expression. loop takes a list or an expression.

Operators:
  + - * / % **                  arithmetic
  == != < > <= >=               comparison
  and or not                    logic, also && || !
  in                            membership in a list or a map
  contains startsWith endsWith  text tests
  matches                       regular expression test
  c ? a : b                     choice
  a ?? b                        a, or b when a is nil
  [] ?.                         read an element or a key
  x[i:j] 1..3                   slice and range
  |                             pipe: vars.name | lower()

Predicates:
  In a predicate, such as the second argument of filter, # is the
  element, and .name reads the key name of a map element:
  filter(vars.users, .uid >= 1000).

Missing values:
  A missing map key is nil. A module argument whose expression gives
  null fails the host.

Quoting:
  Quote a YAML value that starts with a quote, [, {, &, *, or !, or
  that contains ": " or " #".

When they run:
  winch evaluates expressions on the machine that runs winch, during
  the plan. They cannot read the result of a step. winch checks each
  one when it loads the project: an unknown name or a syntax error is
  an error. An error while winch evaluates one for a host fails that
  host. The other hosts go on. $env and now() are errors in every
  expression.

Functions:
  abs            abs(n): the absolute value of the number n.
  all            all(list, pred): true when pred is true for each element.
  any            any(list, pred): true when pred is true for at least one element.
  bitand         bitand(a, b): the bitwise AND of two integers.
  bitnand        bitnand(a, b): the bitwise AND NOT of two integers.
  bitnot         bitnot(a): the bitwise NOT of an integer.
  bitor          bitor(a, b): the bitwise OR of two integers.
  bitshl         bitshl(a, n): a shifted left by n bits.
  bitshr         bitshr(a, n): a shifted right by n bits, with its sign.
  bitushr        bitushr(a, n): a shifted right by n bits, without its sign.
  bitxor         bitxor(a, b): the bitwise XOR of two integers.
  ceil           ceil(n): n rounded up to an integer value.
  concat         concat(a, b, ...): the lists joined into one list.
  count          count(list, pred): the number of elements for which pred is true.
  date           date(s), date(s, layout), or date(s, layout, zone): the time in the text s, such as date("2026-10-30").
  duration       duration(s): the duration in the text s, such as duration("1h30m").
  filter         filter(list, pred): the elements for which pred is true: filter(match("all"), .name != host.name).
  find           find(list, pred): the first element for which pred is true, or nil.
  findIndex      findIndex(list, pred): the index of the first element for which pred is true, or -1.
  findLast       findLast(list, pred): the last element for which pred is true, or nil.
  findLastIndex  findLastIndex(list, pred): the index of the last element for which pred is true, or -1.
  first          first(list): the first element, or nil for an empty list.
  flatten        flatten(list): the elements of the list and of its nested lists, in one list.
  float          float(v): the number or the text v as a float.
  floor          floor(n): n rounded down to an integer value.
  fromBase64     fromBase64(s): the text that the base64 text s encodes.
  fromJSON       fromJSON(s): the value of the JSON text s.
  fromPairs      fromPairs(list): a map from a list of [key, value] pairs.
  get            get(m, key): the value of key in the map m, or nil when m has no such key.
  groupBy        groupBy(list, pred): a map from each result of pred to the list of elements that give it.
  hasPrefix      hasPrefix(s, prefix): true when s starts with prefix.
  hasSuffix      hasSuffix(s, suffix): true when s ends with suffix.
  indexOf        indexOf(s, sub): the index of the first sub in s, or -1.
  int            int(v): the number or the text v as an integer: int("8080").
  join           join(list, sep): the strings of list joined with sep.
  json           json(v): v as JSON on one line.
  keys           keys(m): the keys of the map m, in sorted order.
  last           last(list): the last element, or nil for an empty list.
  lastIndexOf    lastIndexOf(s, sub): the index of the last sub in s, or -1.
  len            len(x): the length of a string, a list, or a map.
  lower          lower(s): s in lower case.
  map            map(list, pred): the result of pred for each element: map(vars.users, .name).
  match          match(pattern): the hosts that match the host pattern, each with name, short, address, groups, and vars, as host has.
  max            max(a, b, ...), or max(list): the largest number.
  mean           mean(list): the average of the numbers.
  median         median(list): the median of the numbers.
  min            min(a, b, ...), or min(list): the smallest number.
  none           none(list, pred): true when pred is false for each element.
  one            one(list, pred): true when pred is true for exactly one element.
  quote          quote(s): s in single quotes for sh, each ' as '\''.
  reduce         reduce(list, pred, initial): one value from all elements.
  repeat         repeat(s, n): s n times.
  replace        replace(s, old, new): s with each old replaced by new.
  reverse        reverse(list): the elements in reverse order.
  round          round(n): n rounded to the nearest integer value.
  sort           sort(list), or sort(list, "desc"): the elements in ascending or descending order.
  sortBy         sortBy(list, pred), or sortBy(list, pred, "desc"): the elements in the order of the results of pred: sortBy(vars.users, .uid).
  split          split(s, sep), or split(s, sep, n): the parts of s between each sep.
  splitAfter     splitAfter(s, sep), or splitAfter(s, sep, n): the parts of s after each sep, each with sep at its end.
  sqlquote       sqlquote(s): s as a MariaDB string literal: in single quotes, each ' as '', each \ as \\.
  string         string(v): v as text: string(8080) is "8080".
  sum            sum(list), or sum(list, pred): the sum of the numbers, or of the results of pred.
  take           take(list, n): the first n elements.
  timezone       timezone(name): the time zone name, such as "Europe/Berlin", for the In method of a date.
  toBase64       toBase64(s): s in base64.
  toPairs        toPairs(m): the [key, value] pairs of the map m, in sorted key order.
  trim           trim(s), or trim(s, chars): s without white space, or without the characters in chars, at both ends.
  trimPrefix     trimPrefix(s, prefix): s without prefix at its start.
  trimSuffix     trimSuffix(s, suffix): s without suffix at its end.
  type           type(v): the type of v as text: nil, bool, int, float, string, array, map, time.Time for a date, or time.Duration.
  uniq           uniq(list): the elements without repeats, in their first order.
  upper          upper(s): s in upper case.
  values         values(m): the values of the map m, in the order of its sorted keys.

Example:
  # winch.yaml
  name: demo
  hosts:
    mail.acme.com: {}
    web1.acme.com: {}
  vars:
    mail_server: false
    domain: acme.com
    admins: [ann, bob]
    mailname: !expr 'vars.mail_server ? vars.domain : host.name'
  steps:
    - name: Set the mail name
      file:
        path: /etc/mailname
        content: !expr 'vars.mailname + "\n"'
      when: !expr vars.mail_server
    - name: Add the admins
      user:
        name: !expr item
        groups: [sudo]
      loop: !expr sort(vars.admins)
    - name: List the other hosts
      file:
        path: /etc/peers
        content: !expr 'join(map(filter(match("all"), .name != host.name), .address), "\n") + "\n"'

