expressions.sortBy: function

sortBy(list, pred), or sortBy(list, pred, "desc"): the elements in the
order of the results of pred: sortBy(vars.users, .uid).
==> winch doc expressions.split
expressions.split: function

split(s, sep), or split(s, sep, n): the parts of s between each sep.
With n, at most n parts.
==> winch doc expressions.splitAfter
expressions.splitAfter: function

splitAfter(s, sep), or splitAfter(s, sep, n): the parts of s after each
sep, each with sep at its end.
==> winch doc expressions.sqlquote
expressions.sqlquote: function

sqlquote(s): s as a MariaDB string literal: in single quotes, each ' as
'', each \ as \\. For PostgreSQL, write E before it. It assumes the
default SQL mode, without NO_BACKSLASH_ESCAPES, and a UTF-8 client.
==> winch doc expressions.string
expressions.string: function

string(v): v as text: string(8080) is "8080".
==> winch doc expressions.sum
expressions.sum: function

sum(list), or sum(list, pred): the sum of the numbers, or of the results
of pred.
==> winch doc expressions.take
expressions.take: function

take(list, n): the first n elements.
==> winch doc expressions.timezone
expressions.timezone: function

timezone(name): the time zone name, such as "Europe/Berlin", for the In
method of a date.
==> winch doc expressions.toBase64
expressions.toBase64: function

toBase64(s): s in base64.
==> winch doc expressions.toPairs
expressions.toPairs: function

toPairs(m): the [key, value] pairs of the map m, in sorted key order.
==> winch doc expressions.trim
expressions.trim: function

trim(s), or trim(s, chars): s without white space, or without the
characters in chars, at both ends.
==> winch doc expressions.trimPrefix
expressions.trimPrefix: function

trimPrefix(s, prefix): s without prefix at its start.
==> winch doc expressions.trimSuffix
expressions.trimSuffix: function

trimSuffix(s, suffix): s without suffix at its end.
==> winch doc expressions.type
expressions.type: function

type(v): the type of v as text: nil, bool, int, float, string, array,
map, time.Time for a date, or time.Duration.
==> winch doc expressions.uniq
expressions.uniq: function

uniq(list): the elements without repeats, in their first order.
==> winch doc expressions.upper
expressions.upper: function

upper(s): s in upper case.
==> winch doc expressions.values
expressions.values: function

values(m): the values of the map m, in the order of its sorted keys.
==> winch doc facts
facts: values that winch reads from each host

Facts are values that winch reads from each host before the plan, such
as the OS release.

Reading a fact:
  Expressions read a fact as facts.os.id, and templates as
  {{ .facts.os.id }}. Facts are a fixed set. A read of an unknown fact
  is an error.

    # winch.yaml
    name: demo
    hosts:
      web1.example.com: {}
    steps:
      - recipe: base
    recipes:
      base:
        require:
          - !expr facts.os.id == "debian" and facts.os.version == "13"
        steps:
          - reboot:
              if_required: true
            when: !expr facts.reboot_required

    # The plan fails a host that is not Debian 13. A host that needs a
    # reboot reboots.

When winch reads them:
  winch reads the facts once per host per run, before the first step,
  and again after a reboot. They describe the host before the run.

Keys:
  os               map      -  OS release of the host
  os.id            string   -  distribution, such as debian or ubuntu
  os.version       string   -  release number, such as 13 or 24.04
  os.codename      string   -  release code name, such as trixie or noble
  os.arch          string   -  CPU architecture
  reboot_required  boolean  -  pending reboot

Run "winch doc facts.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc facts.os
facts.os: map

OS release of the host. winch reads id, version, and codename from
/etc/os-release, or from /usr/lib/os-release when the first does not
exist.

Keys:
  id        string  -  distribution, such as debian or ubuntu
  version   string  -  release number, such as 13 or 24.04
  codename  string  -  release code name, such as trixie or noble
  arch      string  -  CPU architecture
==> winch doc facts.os.id
facts.os.id: string

Distribution, such as debian or ubuntu.
==> winch doc facts.os.version
facts.os.version: string

Release number, such as 13 or 24.04. It is a string: compare it with a
string, such as "13".
==> winch doc facts.os.codename
facts.os.codename: string

Release code name, such as trixie or noble.
==> winch doc facts.os.arch
facts.os.arch: string

CPU architecture: amd64 or arm64.
==> winch doc facts.reboot_required
facts.reboot_required: boolean

Pending reboot. It is true when /run/reboot-required exists, or when a
kernel in /boot is newer than the running kernel.
==> winch doc secrets
secrets: encrypted values in the project

A secret is a value that the project keeps encrypted and winch never
prints.

Format:
  A secret is encrypted with age for the recipients in winch.yaml. The
  !secret tag marks it, followed by the ciphertext in base64 on one
  line.

Where a secret stands:
  A !secret stands only in a variable value: in the vars of an
  inventory, and in the defaults of winch.yaml and of recipes, at any
  depth of a list or a map. --set cannot give a secret.

Commands:
  winch secret init                  make an age key, add it to recipients
  winch secret generate vars.<name>  write a random password into the inventory
  winch secret encrypt               encrypt a value from stdin
  winch secret rekey                 encrypt all secrets for the current recipients

The age key:
  winch secret init makes a key at ~/.config/winch/age.key, or at the
  path in WINCH_AGE_KEY. It makes a key only when no key is there.
  With WINCH_AGE_IDENTITY_CMD, it makes no key and adds the public key
  of the command's key.

  winch reads the age identity from the output of the command in
  WINCH_AGE_IDENTITY_CMD, such as op read op://infra/winch/key.
  Without it, winch reads the file in WINCH_AGE_KEY, or else
  ~/.config/winch/age.key. winch validate needs no identity.

A new member:
  A new member runs winch secret init and sends the change for review.
  The new key can read the secrets after a member who can read them
  runs winch secret rekey.

Output:
  A variable is secret when its value is a !secret, or when its
  default reads a secret variable. The plan, diffs, errors, logs, and
  JSON show (secret) in place of a secret, also a part of a value that
  comes from a secret. A filter replaces each secret value in all text
  that winch prints, also in output from the host. The filter also
  hides private keys, age identities, passwords in URLs, and
  well-known tokens.

Secrets in commands:
  A secret in a command does not show in the process list of the host.
  A program that the command starts with the secret as an argument,
  such as psql -c, does show it.

Warnings and errors:
  - winch warns about text in project files that looks like a private
    key or a token, and is not a !secret.
  - winch warns about a file step whose content reads a secret and
    whose mode is missing or lets others read it.
  - A !secret in a certbot hook is an error: certbot stores its hooks
    in a file that other users can read. A hook that reads a secret
    through match() fails the host.

Example:
  # inventories/production.yaml
  hosts:
    db1.acme.com: {}
  groups:
    all:
      vars:
        db_password: !secret YWdlLWVuY3J5cHRpb24ub3JnL3YxCi0+IFgyNTUxOSB...

  # winch.yaml
  name: acme
  recipients:
    - age1ql3z7hjy54pw3hyww5ayyfg7zqgvc7w3j2elw8zmrj2kg5sfn9aqmcac8p
  steps:
    - file:
        path: /etc/app/db.env
        content: !tpl "DB_PASSWORD={{ .vars.db_password }}\n"
        mode: "0600"
==> winch doc workflow
workflow: how to plan and apply changes

A run of winch plans the changes, asks for approval, and then applies
them.

Plan and apply:
  winch plan shows the changes and changes nothing. winch apply shows
  the same plan, asks, and then makes the changes. For each host, the
  plan connects to the host, reads the facts, evaluates the steps,
  checks the requirements of the recipes, and checks each step on the
  host. Apply checks each step again right before it changes it. The
  second check decides.

Plan marks:
  +  create
  ~  change
  -  remove
  ?  unknown: a cmd without creates or unless
  !  a handler, or a pending then, gate, or assert that will run
  *  a reboot, assert, or health gate that runs during apply

Plan hash:
  The last line of a plan is its hash, such as plan a3f9c2e1d07b4c58.
  The hash covers the project name, the hosts with the host name and
  port that ssh resolves for them and their login users, the stages,
  the evaluated steps, the check results, and the pending handlers and
  then commands. winch apply --check <hash> applies only when the new
  plan has that hash, without the question.

Without a terminal:
  apply without --yes or --check prints the plan and exits 5. Its last
  line is the command that applies that plan.

What winch changes:
  winch changes only what steps declare. Deleting a step changes
  nothing on the host. To remove a thing, declare it with absent:
  true. The plan fails a host when two steps manage the same path,
  package, unit, user, or group with different arguments.

Stopping and resuming:
  The first Ctrl-C lets the running step of each host finish, starts
  no new step, and ends with the summary. A second Ctrl-C closes the
  connections to the hosts. A new run is the resume: steps that already
  succeeded check as ok, and pending handlers run.

Access to the host:
  winch keeps its access to each host.
  - A step that stops, disables, masks, or removes the SSH server
    fails: "this cuts off winch's access".
  - Some steps get the rollback of confirm_access without it: a change
    of the login user's authorized_keys, ~/.ssh, or home directory,
    groups added to the login user, a change of /etc/pam.d, and a
    change of /etc/sudoers or /etc/sudoers.d when the login user is not
    root. Every firewall step gets it too. The plan marks such a step.
    Run winch doc step.confirm_access.
  - A step that changes sshd's config while ssh.socket is active, as on
    Ubuntu, restarts ssh.socket. It gets the same protection.
  - After the last step of a host that changed something, winch checks
    the access once more. When it fails, the host fails, and nothing
    rolls back.

Changing the SSH port:
  The access check connects to the port in your SSH config. So a change
  of sshd's Port in one run always rolls back. Move sshd to a new port
  in three runs:

    1. Let sshd listen on the old and the new port.
    2. Change the port in the SSH config.
    3. Remove the old port from sshd.

Exit codes:
  0  success
  1  one or more hosts failed
  2  plan --exit-code: apply would change something
  3  invalid command line or project; nothing ran
  4  one or more hosts were unreachable, and no step failed
  5  confirmation needed; nothing changed
  6  the plan hash differs from --check; nothing changed

  When several apply, the first in the order 3, 1, 4, 6, 5, 2, 0 wins.

JSON output:
  --json writes one JSON object per line on stdout. Each has type and
  time. The types:

    version     the format version; always first
    diagnostic  an error or a warning, with file, line, col, and message
    planned     the check of one step
    applied     the apply of one step
    host        a host finished, failed, or was unreachable
    exec        the output of winch exec on one host
    summary     the counts, the plan hash, and the exit code; always last

  A step event has host and address, such as mail.acme.com:nginx#3[0]:
  the host, the stage or recipe, the step number from 1, and the loop
  index from 0.

Example:
  winch plan site
  winch apply site
  winch apply site --check a3f9c2e1d07b4c58
  winch plan site --exit-code
==> winch doc assert
assert: stop a host when a check command fails

The plan marks the step with * and runs nothing. Apply runs the command.
A failed assert stays pending on the host, and the next apply runs it
again, also when nothing else changes.

Keys:
  command  string  required  check command
  message  string  required  error when the check fails

Example:
  - name: Check that nginx answers
    assert:
      command: curl -fsS http://localhost/
      message: nginx does not answer on port 80

Run "winch doc assert.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc assert.command
assert.command: string, required

Check command. It runs during apply, through sh -e as root, in /. Exit 0
passes.
==> winch doc assert.message
assert.message: string, required

Error when the check fails. The host fails with it when the command
exits non-zero.
==> winch doc certbot
certbot: get a Let's Encrypt certificate

The step runs certbot certonly. Renewal stays with certbot's own timer.
Install the package certbot in an earlier pkg step. The nginx and apache
authenticators also need python3-certbot-nginx or
python3-certbot-apache.

Keys:
  name           string                     required  certificate name in /etc/letsencrypt
  domains        string or list of strings  required  no wildcards
  email          string                     required  Let's Encrypt account address
  authenticator  string                     nginx     challenge plugin
  webroot_path   string                     -         web root for webroot
  pre_hook       string                     -         command before each try
  post_hook      string                     -         command after each try
  deploy_hook    string                     -         command after each new certificate
  reuse_key      boolean                    false     keep the private key at renewal

Example:
  - certbot:
      name: mail
      domains: [mail.example.com]
      email: admin@example.com
      deploy_hook: systemctl reload postfix

Run "winch doc certbot.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc certbot.name
certbot.name: string, required

Certificate name in /etc/letsencrypt.
==> winch doc certbot.domains
certbot.domains: string or list of strings, required

No wildcards. A wildcard needs a DNS challenge.
==> winch doc certbot.email
certbot.email: string, required

Let's Encrypt account address.
==> winch doc certbot.authenticator
certbot.authenticator: string, default nginx

Challenge plugin: nginx, apache, standalone, or webroot. The nginx and
apache plugins do not change the config of the web server. standalone
binds port 80 itself; pre_hook and post_hook can stop and start what
holds that port. webroot needs webroot_path.
==> winch doc certbot.webroot_path
certbot.webroot_path: string

Web root for webroot.
==> winch doc certbot.pre_hook
certbot.pre_hook: string

Command before each try to get a certificate. certbot stores it in a
file that other users can read, so it cannot hold a secret. A hook that
needs a secret reads it from a root-only file that a file step writes.
==> winch doc certbot.post_hook
certbot.post_hook: string

Command after each try to get a certificate. certbot stores it in a file
that other users can read, so it cannot hold a secret.
==> winch doc certbot.deploy_hook
certbot.deploy_hook: string

Command after each new certificate, also the first. A failed hook fails
the step, and the next apply runs it again. It cannot hold a secret.
==> winch doc certbot.reuse_key
certbot.reuse_key: boolean, default false

Keep the private key at renewal.
==> winch doc cmd
cmd: run a command

Without creates or unless, the check result is unknown, and apply runs
the command each time. A secret in the command does not show in the
process list of the host. A program that the command starts with the
secret as an argument, such as psql -c, does show it.

Keys:
  command  string   required  shell script
  creates  string   -         path that marks the work as done
  unless   string   -         check command; exit 0 skips the step
  user     string   root      account that runs command
  dir      string   /         working directory
  timeout  integer  600       limit in seconds, also for unless

Example:
  - name: Build the alias database
    cmd:
      command: newaliases
      creates: /etc/aliases.db

Run "winch doc cmd.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc cmd.command
cmd.command: string, required

Shell script. It runs through sh -e as root, in /, with stdin from
/dev/null. A script of several lines stops at its first failed line.
Without creates or unless, the check result is unknown.
==> winch doc cmd.creates
cmd.creates: string

Path that marks the work as done. The step is ok when the path exists.
When the command exits 0 and the path is still missing, the step fails.
The path has no glob characters. A step has at most one of creates and
unless.
==> winch doc cmd.unless
cmd.unless: string

Check command; exit 0 skips the step. It runs at the check as root, in
/, with the timeout of the step. user and dir do not apply to it. A step
has at most one of creates and unless.
==> winch doc cmd.user
cmd.user: string, default root

Account that runs command. Its home is HOME. The step starts no login
session: PAM does not run, so the login umask and limits of the account
do not apply, and the umask is 022.
==> winch doc cmd.dir
cmd.dir: string, default /

Working directory.
==> winch doc cmd.timeout
cmd.timeout: integer, default 600

Limit in seconds, also for unless. After it, winch stops the command and
the step fails.
==> winch doc debconf
debconf: set a debconf answer

Only the scripts of a package read its answers. Put the step before the
pkg step that installs the package, also when the package comes in as a
dependency.

Keys:
  package   string  required  package that asks the question
  question  string  required  name, such as postfix/main_mailer_type
  type      string  required  question type, such as string, boolean, or select
  value     string  required  answer, as text

Example:
  - debconf:
      package: postfix
      question: postfix/main_mailer_type
      type: select
      value: Internet Site

Run "winch doc debconf.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc debconf.package
debconf.package: string, required

Package that asks the question.
==> winch doc debconf.question
debconf.question: string, required

Name, such as postfix/main_mailer_type.
==> winch doc debconf.type
debconf.type: string, required

Question type, such as string, boolean, or select.
==> winch doc debconf.value
debconf.value: string, required

Answer, as text. Only the scripts of the package read it, so put the
step before the pkg step that installs the package.
==> winch doc dir
dir: manage a directory

The step creates missing parent directories, owned by root with mode
0755. winch checks owner, group, and mode on every run.

Keys:
  path     string           required  absolute
  owner    name or ID       root
  group    name or ID       root
  mode     string           0755      octal, in quotes
  recurse  boolean          false     owner and group for the whole tree
  absent   boolean          false     remove it with its content
  replace  boolean          false     remove another kind of thing at path first
  purge    boolean          false     remove entries that nothing declares
  keep     list of strings  -         name patterns that purge keeps

Example:
  - dir:
      path: /var/www/app
      owner: www-data
      group: www-data
      mode: "0750"

Run "winch doc dir.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc dir.path
dir.path: string, required

Absolute. The step creates missing parent directories, owned by root
with mode 0755. It follows a symbolic link to a directory and manages
the directory behind it.
==> winch doc dir.owner
dir.owner: name or ID, default root

A user name, or a numeric ID that needs no account. A value of only
digits is an ID, also in quotes.
==> winch doc dir.group
dir.group: name or ID, default root

A group name, or a numeric ID that needs no group. A value of only
digits is an ID, also in quotes.
==> winch doc dir.mode
dir.mode: string, default 0755

Octal, in quotes: "0750". Without quotes, YAML reads it as a number.
==> winch doc dir.recurse
dir.recurse: boolean, default false

Owner and group for the whole tree. The mode stays the mode of the
directory alone.
==> winch doc dir.absent
dir.absent: boolean, default false

Remove it with its content.
==> winch doc dir.replace
dir.replace: boolean, default false

Remove another kind of thing at path first, such as a file or a link.
Without it, such a thing fails the check.
==> winch doc dir.purge
dir.purge: boolean, default false

Remove entries that nothing declares. Purge looks only at the direct
entries. It keeps an entry that a package owns, or that a step for the
host writes. Each removal is a change.
==> winch doc dir.keep
dir.keep: list of strings

Name patterns that purge keeps, such as *.db. They are glob patterns.
keep needs purge.
==> winch doc download
download: download a file or an archive

The host fetches the file and checks its SHA-256. A download with
another checksum fails and keeps the old file. A step has path or
unpack.

Keys:
  url     string      required  fetched by the host
  sha256  string      required  checksum of the file
  path    string      -         absolute target of a single file
  unpack  string      -         directory for the unpacked archive
  owner   name or ID  root      of the file or the unpacked tree
  group   name or ID  root      of the file or the unpacked tree
  mode    string      0644      octal, in quotes; path only

Example:
  - download:
      url: https://example.com/releases/app-1.4.2-linux-amd64
      sha256: 9f86d081884c7d659a2feaa0c55ad015a3bf4f1b2b0b822cd15d6c15b0f00a08
      path: /usr/local/bin/app
      mode: "0755"

Run "winch doc download.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc download.url
download.url: string, required

Fetched by the host.
==> winch doc download.sha256
download.sha256: string, required

Checksum of the file. Take the value from the checksum file that the
vendor publishes, or download the file once and run sha256sum on it. A
download with another checksum fails and keeps the old file.
==> winch doc download.path
download.path: string

Absolute target of a single file. A step has path or unpack.
==> winch doc download.unpack
download.unpack: string

Directory for the unpacked archive. The path of the URL ends in .tar.gz,
.tgz, .tar.xz, or .zip. The step unpacks again when sha256, owner,
group, or the directory changes. A new archive replaces the whole tree.
Keep the files that a program writes, such as its config, data, and
logs, outside the directory.
==> winch doc download.owner
download.owner: name or ID, default root

Of the file or the unpacked tree. A user name or a numeric ID.
==> winch doc download.group
download.group: name or ID, default root

Of the file or the unpacked tree. A group name or a numeric ID.
==> winch doc download.mode
download.mode: string, default 0644

Octal, in quotes; path only. Without quotes, YAML reads it as a number.
With unpack, mode is an error: the entries keep the modes of the
archive, without write permission for group and others.
==> winch doc file
file: manage a file

At most one of content, template, source, copy_from, and stock sets the
content. A step without any of them manages only the owner, group, and
mode that it gives, of a file that exists. The step creates missing
parent directories, owned by root with mode 0755.

Keys:
  path       string      required  absolute
  content    string      -         literal text
  template   string      -         file in the recipe's templates/
  source     string      -         file in the recipe's files/
  copy_from  string      -         file on the host
  stock      boolean     false     content that the package ships
  owner      name or ID  root
  group      name or ID  root
  mode       string      0644      octal, in quotes
  validate   string      -         check command for the new file
  absent     boolean     false
  replace    boolean     false     remove another kind of thing at path first

Example:
  - name: Write the message of the day
    file:
      path: /etc/motd
      content: "Welcome to mail.example.com.\n"
      mode: "0644"

Run "winch doc file.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc file.path
file.path: string, required

Absolute. The step creates missing parent directories, owned by root
with mode 0755.
==> winch doc file.content
file.content: string

Literal text. A step without content, template, source, copy_from, or
stock sets only the owner, group, and mode that it gives, with no
defaults, of a file that exists. When that file is missing, the plan
says does not exist yet. Apply fails when it is still missing.
==> winch doc file.template
file.template: string

File in the recipe's templates/ directory. Its output is the content.
==> winch doc file.source
file.source: string

File in the recipe's files/ directory. Its bytes are the content.
==> winch doc file.copy_from
file.copy_from: string

File on the host. Its bytes are the content, and its permission bits are
the default mode. The default owner and group stay root: the owner of
the source does not carry over.
==> winch doc file.stock
file.stock: boolean, default false

Content that the package ships for path, from the installed version. The
package also gives the default owner, group, and mode.
==> winch doc file.owner
file.owner: name or ID, default root

A user name or a numeric ID. A step without content has no default.
==> winch doc file.group
file.group: name or ID, default root

A group name or a numeric ID. A step without content has no default.
==> winch doc file.mode
file.mode: string, default 0644

Octal, in quotes: "0640". Without quotes, YAML reads it as a number.
With stock, the package gives the default. With copy_from, the source
gives it. A step without content has no default.
==> winch doc file.validate
file.validate: string

Check command for the new file. The command names the real path, or
checks the whole config: nginx -t. winch puts the new file in place and
runs the command. When the command fails, the old file comes back and
the step fails.
==> winch doc file.absent
file.absent: boolean, default false

Remove the file. A path of another kind fails the check.
==> winch doc file.replace
file.replace: boolean, default false

Remove another kind of thing at path first, such as a directory. Without
it, such a thing fails the check.
==> winch doc firewall
firewall: filter incoming traffic

The step owns the nftables table inet winch and changes no other table.
The rules of Docker, fail2ban, or /etc/nftables.conf stay. Its input
chain filters traffic to the host. A host has at most one firewall step
in a run.

The step needs nft: install the package nftables in an earlier pkg step.

Every change gets the SSH check and the rollback of confirm_access. When
no new SSH connection works after the new rules load, the old rules come
back.

Keys:
  allow   list of maps  -      accepted traffic; the host drops the rest
  absent  boolean       false  remove the table, its file, and its unit

Example:
  - firewall:
      allow:
        - tcp: [22, 80, 443]
        - udp: 51820
        - tcp: 5432
          from: [10.0.0.0/8, 2001:db8::/32]

Run "winch doc firewall.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc firewall.allow
firewall.allow: list of maps

Accepted traffic; the host drops the rest. It always accepts established
and related traffic, loopback, ICMP echo requests, the ICMPv6 types that
IPv6 needs, and DHCP replies. It drops invalid packets.

Keys of each element:
  tcp   integer or list of integers  -  ports
  udp   integer or list of integers  -  ports
  from  string or list of strings    -  source addresses or networks
==> winch doc firewall.allow.tcp
firewall.allow.tcp: integer or list of integers

Ports. A rule has tcp or udp.
==> winch doc firewall.allow.udp
firewall.allow.udp: integer or list of integers

Ports. A rule has tcp or udp.
==> winch doc firewall.allow.from
firewall.allow.from: string or list of strings

Source addresses or networks. IPv4 and IPv6 are allowed. Without from,
the rule accepts every source.
==> winch doc firewall.absent
firewall.absent: boolean, default false

Remove the table, its file, and its unit.
==> winch doc group
group: manage a group

Keys:
  name    string   required
  gid     ID       -
  system  boolean  false     system group, at creation only
  absent  boolean  false

Example:
  - group:
      name: deploy
      system: true

Run "winch doc group.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc group.name
group.name: string, required

A new group's name has lowercase letters, digits, _, and -, and does not
start with a digit or -. It has at most 32 characters.
==> winch doc group.gid
group.gid: ID

A whole number. A gid that another group has fails the check.
==> winch doc group.system
group.system: boolean, default false

System group, at creation only. It counts only when the step creates the
group.
==> winch doc group.absent
group.absent: boolean, default false

Remove the group. The check fails when the group is the primary group of
a user.
==> winch doc hostname
hostname: set the host name

Keys:
  name  string  required  short name, such as mail

Example:
  - hostname:
      name: mail

Run "winch doc hostname.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc hostname.name
hostname.name: string, required

Short name, such as mail.
==> winch doc line
line: manage one line in a file

Keys:
  path      string   required  absolute
  line      string   -         line to keep
  match     string   required  regular expression for the line
  validate  string   -         check command, as for file
  absent    boolean  false     remove the matching lines

Example:
  - line:
      path: /etc/default/grub
      match: ^GRUB_TIMEOUT=
      line: GRUB_TIMEOUT=2
    then: update-grub

Run "winch doc line.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc line.path
line.path: string, required

Absolute. A missing file is a change in the plan, since an earlier step
can create it. Apply fails when it is still missing.
==> winch doc line.line
line.line: string

Line to keep. It replaces the one line that match finds, or goes at the
end of the file.
==> winch doc line.match
line.match: string, required

Regular expression for the line. More than one matching line fails the
check.
==> winch doc line.validate
line.validate: string

Check command, as for file. It runs with absent too.
==> winch doc line.absent
line.absent: boolean, default false

Remove the matching lines.
==> winch doc link
link: manage a symbolic link

The step creates missing parent directories, owned by root with mode
0755.

Keys:
  path      string   required  absolute
  target    string   -         relative or absolute; need not exist
  validate  string   -         check command, as for file
  absent    boolean  false
  replace   boolean  false     remove another kind of thing at path first

Example:
  - link:
      path: /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/app
      target: /etc/nginx/sites-available/app

Run "winch doc link.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc link.path
link.path: string, required

Absolute. The step creates missing parent directories, owned by root
with mode 0755.
==> winch doc link.target
link.target: string

Relative or absolute; need not exist.
==> winch doc link.validate
link.validate: string

Check command, as for file.
==> winch doc link.absent
link.absent: boolean, default false

Remove the link. A path of another kind fails the check.
==> winch doc link.replace
link.replace: boolean, default false

Remove another kind of thing at path first, such as a file or a
directory. Without it, such a thing fails the check.
==> winch doc pkg
pkg: install or remove packages

Before the first install of a run on a host, apply refreshes the package
index. After a repo step changed something, the next pkg step refreshes
it again.

Keys:
  name        string or list of strings  required  installed in one apt call
  recommends  boolean                    true      install recommended packages too
  purge       boolean                    false     remove the config files too
  absent      boolean                    false

Example:
  - pkg:
      name: [nginx, certbot]
      recommends: false

Run "winch doc pkg.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc pkg.name
pkg.name: string or list of strings, required

Installed in one apt call.
==> winch doc pkg.recommends
pkg.recommends: boolean, default true

Install recommended packages too.
==> winch doc pkg.purge
pkg.purge: boolean, default false

Remove the config files too. purge needs absent.
==> winch doc pkg.absent
pkg.absent: boolean, default false

Remove the packages.
==> winch doc reboot
reboot: reboot the host

Keys:
  if_required  boolean  required  only on a pending reboot
  timeout      integer  600       wait limit in seconds

Example:
  - reboot:
      if_required: true

Run "winch doc reboot.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc reboot.if_required
reboot.if_required: boolean, required

Only on a pending reboot. With false, the step always reboots.
==> winch doc reboot.timeout
reboot.timeout: integer, default 600

Wait limit in seconds.
==> winch doc repo
repo: manage an apt source

Keys:
  name         string                     required  file name in /etc/apt
  url          string                     required  without a user or a password
  suite        string                     required  such as trixie
  components   string or list of strings  -         such as main
  key          string                     -         key file in the recipe's files/
  key_content  string                     -         key text, in place of key

Example:
  - repo:
      name: nginx
      url: https://nginx.org/packages/debian
      suite: trixie
      components: [nginx]
      key: key.asc

Run "winch doc repo.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc repo.name
repo.name: string, required

File name in /etc/apt, of the source file and of the key file.
==> winch doc repo.url
repo.url: string, required

Without a user or a password.
==> winch doc repo.suite
repo.suite: string, required

Such as trixie. A flat suite ends in /.
==> winch doc repo.components
repo.components: string or list of strings

Such as main. They are required, except for a flat suite, which has
none.
==> winch doc repo.key
repo.key: string

Key file in the recipe's files/ directory: an ASCII-armored OpenPGP
public key. Download the vendor's key once and keep it in the project. A
step has at most one of key and key_content.
==> winch doc repo.key_content
repo.key_content: string

Key text, in place of key: an ASCII-armored OpenPGP public key. A step
has at most one of key and key_content.
==> winch doc service
service: control a systemd unit

With running: false, a missing unit file is ok, with the note <unit>:
unit file not found. A service step with confirm_access: true checks
that a new SSH connection works after the step, and rolls back when none
does. Run winch doc step.confirm_access. In a handler, restarted and
reloaded check as ok when a service step of the run started the unit
after the handler's last notify, no earlier step of the handler changed
anything, and the step has no confirm_access.

Keys:
  name       string   required  unit, with or without .service
  running    boolean  -         start or stop; unset leaves it
  enabled    boolean  -         start at boot; unset leaves it
  reloaded   boolean  false     reload, in handlers and winch run only
  restarted  boolean  false     restart, in handlers and winch run only

Example:
  - service:
      name: nginx
      running: true
      enabled: true

Run "winch doc service.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc service.name
service.name: string, required

Unit, with or without .service, such as nginx or certbot.timer.
==> winch doc service.running
service.running: boolean

Start or stop; unset leaves it. Without running, the step does not start
or stop the unit.
==> winch doc service.enabled
service.enabled: boolean

Start at boot; unset leaves it. Without enabled, the step does not
change it.
==> winch doc service.reloaded
service.reloaded: boolean, default false

Reload, in handlers and winch run only. A unit that cannot reload
restarts.
==> winch doc service.restarted
service.restarted: boolean, default false

Restart, in handlers and winch run only. A stopped unit stays stopped.
==> winch doc timezone
timezone: set the time zone

Keys:
  name  string  required  zone name, such as Europe/Berlin

Example:
  - timezone:
      name: Europe/Berlin

Run "winch doc timezone.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc timezone.name
timezone.name: string, required

Zone name, such as Europe/Berlin.
==> winch doc unit
unit: manage a systemd unit file

A change of the file, also of its owner or mode, runs systemctl
daemon-reload. daemon-reload does not restart a running unit. A handler
with restarted: true does.

Keys:
  name     string  required  file name with its type
  source   string  -         file in the recipe's files/
  content  string  -         unit file text

Example:
  - unit:
      name: app.service
      content: |
        [Unit]
        Description=App

        [Service]
        ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/app

        [Install]
        WantedBy=multi-user.target

Run "winch doc unit.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc unit.name
unit.name: string, required

File name with its type, such as app.service or app@.service.
==> winch doc unit.source
unit.source: string

File in the recipe's files/ directory. A step has source or content.
==> winch doc unit.content
unit.content: string

Unit file text. A step has source or content.
==> winch doc upgrade
upgrade: upgrade the installed packages

The step refreshes the package index, also during the plan, and checks
for pending upgrades.

Keys:
  mode  string  required  safe or full

Example:
  - upgrade:
      mode: safe

Run "winch doc upgrade.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc upgrade.mode
upgrade.mode: string, required

Safe or full. safe installs the upgrades that remove no package. full
also installs the upgrades that add or remove packages.
==> winch doc user
user: manage a user account

A step that creates a user also creates its home directory. Without
group, it also creates a group with the name of the user. The defaults
and system count only when the step creates the user.

Keys:
  name             string           required
  uid              ID               -
  group            name or ID       <name>        primary group
  system           boolean          false         system user, at creation only
  home             string           /home/<name>  created when missing; never moved
  shell            string           /bin/sh       login shell
  groups           list of strings  -             exact supplementary groups
  add_groups       list of strings  -             groups to join, keeping the others
  password         string           -             crypt hash, usually a !secret
  comment          string           -             full name or description
  authorized_keys  list of strings  -             exact SSH keys of the user
  absent           boolean          false         remove the user; the home stays

Example:
  - user:
      name: deploy
      groups: [www-data]
      shell: /bin/bash
      authorized_keys:
        - ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIGlrZDl2Q3h5c3d6bW9ja2tleWZvcmRvY3MxMjM0 deploy@laptop

Run "winch doc user.<key>" for the full text of a key.
==> winch doc user.name
user.name: string, required

A new user's name has lowercase letters, digits, _, and -, and does not
start with a digit or -. It has at most 32 characters.
==> winch doc user.uid
user.uid: ID

A whole number. A uid that another user has fails the check.
==> winch doc user.group
user.group: name or ID, default <name>

Primary group. Without group, a step that creates the user also creates
a group with the name of the user.
==> winch doc user.system
user.system: boolean, default false

System user, at creation only. Its home is /nonexistent and its shell is
/usr/sbin/nologin. It counts only when the step creates the user.
==> winch doc user.home
user.home: string, default /home/<name>

Created when missing; never moved. The step creates a missing home
directory, except /nonexistent. The default counts only when the step
creates the user. A system user gets /nonexistent. A changed home is not
moved.
==> winch doc user.shell
user.shell: string, default /bin/sh

Login shell. The default counts only when the step creates the user. A
system user gets /usr/sbin/nologin.
==> winch doc user.groups
user.groups: list of strings

Exact supplementary groups. A step has groups or add_groups.
==> winch doc user.add_groups
user.add_groups: list of strings

Groups to join, keeping the others. A step with only name and add_groups
does not create the user: apply fails when the user does not exist.
==> winch doc user.password
user.password: string

Crypt hash, usually a !secret, such as one from mkpasswd -m yescrypt. !
or * alone locks the password. Without password, the password of a new
user is locked.
==> winch doc user.comment
user.comment: string

Full name or description.
==> winch doc user.authorized_keys
user.authorized_keys: list of strings

Exact SSH keys of the user, in ~/.ssh/authorized_keys.
==> winch doc user.absent
user.absent: boolean, default false

Remove the user; the home stays.
